.\" Written from docs/index.html by go run ./tools/man; edit the manual, not this.
.TH CONN 1 "" "conn 0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conn \- a station for directing many hands on one machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conn
.br
.B conn down
.br
.B conn theme claude
.br
.B conn theme vim
.br
.B conn \-\-light
.br
.B conn \-\-dark
.br
.B conn \-\-help
.br
.B conn \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
The station shows what each hand is doing and which one is waiting, and puts the
operator in front of it.
.SH GENERAL
.TP
1-1.
\fBPURPOSE.\fR This manual describes conn and its operation.
.TP
1-2.
\fBSCOPE.\fR The station serves one operator directing many hands on one
machine. It shows the processes running in each project, what each is doing, and
which one is waiting for the operator, and it puts the operator in front of any
of them. It does not edit files, run builds, or hold conversations. The hands
do.
.TP
1-3.
\fBDEFINITIONS.\fR Terms used in this manual.
.RS
.TP
\fBOperator\fR
The person using conn.
.TP
\fBHand\fR
A process conn watches: a shell, an AI, or a command a shell is running. Also
called a station hand.
.TP
\fBAI\fR
An artificial intelligence: an assistant running in a terminal, such as Claude
Code, that works a place and can stop to wait for the operator.
.TP
\fBPlace\fR
The project directory a hand runs in: a git repository, or a folder that holds
several repositories.
.TP
\fBStanding\fR
The state conn reports for a hand: WORKING, ACTIVE, IDLE, or WAITING.
.TP
\fBWaiting\fR
The standing of an AI that has stopped and needs an answer from the operator,
such as a permission or a question.
.TP
\fBContact\fR
A conversation with an AI, from raising it to ending it. Its record is the
transcript.
.TP
\fBConsole\fR
The page conn comes up on. It reads out the machine and gives a verdict.
.TP
\fBRail\fR
The left pane of the window, forty-four columns wide. The watch and the other
views are shown there.
.TP
\fBWatch\fR
The list of hands, grouped by place, in the order they started.
.TP
\fBSlot\fR
The right pane of the window. It shows one hand.
.TP
\fBLook\fR
A page on one row of the watch, shown in the slot.
.TP
\fBList\fR
Every project under the roots, running or not, shown on the rail.
.TP
\fBPicker\fR
The conversations suspended at a place, shown on the rail.
.TP
\fBBar\fR
The status line at the bottom of the window. It shows the state of the keys on
the left and one lamp per hand on the right.
.RE
.TP
1-4.
\fBINDICATIONS.\fR The station indicates two things: what each hand is doing,
and which hand is waiting. The watch and the lamps on the bar show the first.
The word WAITING on the watch, blinking, and a lamp on the bar in the same color
show the second; \fBtab\fR goes to it (4-3).
.TP
1-5.
\fBCOMMANDS.\fR The command alone brings up the console and continues to the
watch. Other commands are given by name after it. \fB\-\-light\fR and
\fB\-\-dark\fR set the ground, ahead of a command if one is given.
.PP
.I "SYNOPSIS"
.RS
.TP
\fBconn\fR
Brings up the console, then the watch.
.TP
\fBconn down\fR
Takes the server down with everything in it, and reports what went.
.TP
\fBconn theme claude\fR
Writes conn's theme for Claude Code.
.TP
\fBconn theme vim\fR
Writes conn's colorscheme for nvim.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-light\fR
Sets the ground light.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-dark\fR
Sets the ground dark.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-help\fR
Prints this table.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-version\fR
Prints the build.
.RE
.SH THE STATION
.TP
2-1.
\fBARRANGEMENT.\fR The station is one window of a tmux server that conn holds.
The rail is on the left and the slot on the right. The bar runs under both.
Figure 2-1 shows the arrangement.
.PP
.RS
.nf
┌──────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┐
│ RAIL                         │ SLOT                        │
│                              │                             │
│ KIND   COMMAND    AGE STATUS │ The hand reached from the   │
│                              │ rail: a shell, an AI, a run │
│ w0zro/conn                   │ or the look. The keys are   │
│ SHELL  zsh        2H    IDLE │ here while a hand is.       │
│ ▸ AI   claude     1H WAITING │                             │
│                              │                             │
├──────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┤
│ BAR  PREFIX                                    ● ● ● ●   │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I "FIGURE 2-1. STATION ARRANGEMENT"
.TP
2-2.
\fBTHE SERVER.\fR conn holds a tmux server of its own. Its socket is under
\fB~/.local/state/conn\fR, or where \fBCONN_SOCKET\fR says. The first \fBconn\fR
brings the server up and puts the terminal on it. A later \fBconn\fR attaches to
what is there. Everything in the server stays until the server is taken down
(5-2). A hand that leaves the slot goes to a window of its own, out of sight,
and keeps running.
.TP
2-3.
\fBCOMING UP.\fR To bring up the station:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
Type \fBconn\fR.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
The console comes up. It reads out the machine, runs the start-up checks, and
gives its verdict: ALL SYSTEMS NOMINAL, or the count of systems that are not.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
Press any key. The watch comes up on the rail and the slot opens beside it.
.RE
.TP
2-4.
\fBTHE CONSOLE.\fR \fBc\fR on the watch brings the console back over the whole
window. Any key returns to the watch.
.TP
2-5.
\fBTHE PREFIX.\fR Chords are given under \fBctrl\-space\fR. \fBCONN_PREFIX\fR
names another prefix, in the spelling tmux uses. While a chord is open, PREFIX
is lit on the bar. No other tmux key is bound.
.RS
.TP
\fBctrl\-space \-\fR
Puts the keys on the rail, from anywhere in the server.
.TP
\fBctrl\-space p\fR
Opens the list, from anywhere in the server (3-7).
.TP
\fBctrl\-space tab\fR
Goes to the AI that has waited longest (4-3).
.TP
\fBctrl\-space ctrl\-space\fR
Goes to the hand that was last in the slot (4-4).
.TP
\fBctrl\-space q\fR
Detaches (2-6).
.RE
.TP
2-6.
\fBSTANDING DOWN.\fR \fBq\fR on the rail detaches the terminal from the server.
\fBctrl\-space q\fR does the same from anywhere in it. The server and every hand
in it keep running, and the next \fBconn\fR attaches to them. Ending the server
is procedure 5-2.
.TP
2-7.
\fBWITHOUT TMUX.\fR Without tmux on the path, conn shows the console and the
watch and reaches nothing. On macOS the watch needs \fBlsof\fR, which is how it
reads where a process is working.
.TP
2-8.
\fBTHE GROUND.\fR The server comes up on a dark or a light ground. The first
time it rises it asks the terminal for its background and keeps the answer for
the life of the server. \fBconn \-\-light\fR and \fBconn \-\-dark\fR set the
ground instead, for a server coming up or one already up. Every pane takes the
new ground.
.TP
2-9.
\fBTHEMES.\fR A program that draws its own colors takes none of the server's.
\fBconn theme claude\fR writes a theme for Claude Code and offers to select it.
\fBconn theme vim\fR writes a colorscheme for nvim, taken with \fBcolorscheme
conn\fR. Each is drawn on the ground the server is on.
.TP
2-10.
\fBENVIRONMENT.\fR The station reads three variables.
.RS
.TP
\fBCONN_ROOTS\fR
Where the list looks for projects, as a path list. \fB~/projects\fR when unset.
.TP
\fBCONN_SOCKET\fR
Where the server's socket is.
.TP
\fBCONN_PREFIX\fR
The prefix for chords, in the spelling tmux uses. \fBC\-Space\fR when unset.
.RE
.SH WATCH PROCEDURES
.TP
3-1.
\fBTHE WATCH.\fR The watch lists the hands by place. A place is titled by its
path below the root; a place outside every root is written from \fB~\fR. Under a
place the hands are shown as a tree: a shell, and what it runs indented under
it. Each row shows the kind, the command, the age, and the standing. On the
console, which is wider, the terminal is shown too. A row keeps its position for
as long as its hand lives, oldest first, and a new row goes on the end. With
nothing running the watch says NOTHING ON WATCH.
.TP
3-2.
\fBSTANDINGS.\fR The last column of a row is its standing.
.RS
.TP
\fBWORKING\fR
Did something between one reading and the next. An AI is WORKING when it is
mid-turn, or when a command is running under it.
.TP
\fBACTIVE\fR
Alive and doing nothing.
.TP
\fBIDLE\fR
A shell at its prompt, or an AI whose turn is over. Nothing is pending.
.TP
\fBWAITING\fR
An AI stopped on something it needs from the operator. The word blinks.
.TP
\fBSTOPPED\fR
Suspended. Shown as a chip.
.TP
\fBENDED\fR
Finished and not yet collected. Shown as a chip.
.RE
.TP
3-3.
\fBWHO IS WAITING.\fR Only an AI that says its own standing is ever WAITING.
Claude Code says it, and says when its standing changed. An AI conn cannot ask
reads as WORKING or ACTIVE from the processor time it spends. conn does not
guess.
.TP
3-4.
\fBKEYS ON THE WATCH.\fR The cursor is on one row. It follows its hand from one
reading to the next.
.RS
.TP
\fBj, k\fR
Move the cursor. The arrows do the same.
.TP
\fBenter\fR
Puts the hand under the cursor in the slot, with the keys in it.
.TP
\fBtab\fR
Goes to the AI that has waited longest (4-3).
.TP
\fBs\fR
Opens a shell at the place under the cursor.
.TP
\fBa\fR
Raises an AI at the place under the cursor (4-2).
.TP
\fBalt\-a\fR
Opens the picker over the conversations suspended at the place (4-5).
.TP
\fBi\fR
Opens the look on the row under the cursor. Pressed again, closes it (3-6).
.TP
\fBx\fR
Asks to end the hand under the cursor (4-6).
.TP
\fBp\fR
Opens the list (3-7).
.TP
\fBc\fR
Brings the console back over the window (2-4).
.TP
\fBq\fR
Detaches (2-6).
.RE
.TP
3-5.
\fBHELD AND REPORTED.\fR A row conn holds a pane for is in the ink, and
\fBenter\fR reaches it. A row conn can only report, a terminal it did not open,
is dimmed. The cursor goes to it and nothing else does. The row in the slot
carries its kind in orange. Outside the server every row is reported.
.TP
3-6.
\fBTHE LOOK.\fR The look is a page on one row, shown in the slot. It says what
the row has no room for: the whole command, the directory, the time up and the
processor time spent, what runs it and what it runs; of an AI, the conversation
it is carrying and what it is stopped on; of the place, what git says of it. To
read a row:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
Put the cursor on the row.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
Press \fBi\fR. The look opens in the slot. The keys stay on the rail.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
\fBj\fR and \fBk\fR move the look with the cursor. \fBtab\fR carries it to the
AI that is waiting.
.TP
\fB4.\fR
Press \fBi\fR again. The look closes. Where the row is one conn holds, its hand
takes the slot.
.RE
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
\fBconn look 1234\fR, from a shell in the server, pins the look to one process
instead of the cursor.
.TP
3-7.
\fBTHE LIST.\fR The list is every project under the roots, whether anything is
running in it or not. A folder that holds several repositories is a row with
them under it. To open a hand at a project:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
Press \fBp\fR on the watch, \fBalt\-p\fR from any view, or \fBctrl\-space p\fR
from anywhere in the server.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
Type to narrow the rows. \fBbackspace\fR and \fBctrl\-u\fR edit the line. The
arrows, or \fBctrl\-p\fR and \fBctrl\-n\fR, move the cursor.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
Press \fBenter\fR. A shell opens at the project and the watch returns with it in
the slot. \fBctrl\-a\fR raises an AI there instead (4-2). \fBalt\-a\fR opens the
picker (4-5).
.TP
\fB4.\fR
\fBesc\fR returns to the watch with nothing opened.
.RE
.TP
3-8.
\fBTHE BAR.\fR The bar is dark at rest. The left half lights for a state of the
keys. The right half is one lamp per row of the watch, in the order of the
watch.
.RS
.TP
\fBPREFIX\fR
A chord is open. The next key is one of 2-5.
.TP
\fBCOPY\fR
The pane with the keys is in copy mode.
.TP
\fBCONFIRM\fR
A kill is armed. The question stands beside it (4-6).
.TP
\fBLamp, gray\fR
The row is WORKING.
.TP
\fBLamp, faint\fR
The row is ACTIVE or IDLE.
.TP
\fBLamp, blinking\fR
The row is WAITING, in the color the word is.
.RE
.SH CONTACT PROCEDURES
.TP
4-1.
\fBCONTACT.\fR A contact is a conversation with an AI, from raising it to ending
it, and its record is the transcript the AI writes. conn raises Claude Code, and
the procedures of this section are written for it. Another AI on the watch is a
hand like any other: it is reached, put in the slot, and ended the same way.
conn cannot ask it its standing, so it is never WAITING.
.TP
4-2.
\fBRAISING AN AI.\fR To start an AI at a place:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
On the watch, put the cursor on any row of the place. Or open the list (3-7) and
put the cursor on the project.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
Press \fBa\fR. On the list, press \fBctrl\-a\fR.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
A shell opens at the place running \fBclaude\fR, takes the slot, and has the
keys. Its row is on the watch under the place, kind AI.
.TP
\fB4.\fR
conn tells the AI where it is: that it runs in a pane of the station, and how to
start anything long-lived in a window of its own, so the watch shows it and the
operator can reach it.
.RE
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
What conn tells the AI travels with conn. Nothing is written into the settings
of the AI.
.TP
4-3.
\fBANSWERING AN AI.\fR When an AI stops on something it needs from the operator,
its row reads WAITING, blinking, and its lamp on the bar blinks with it. To
answer it:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
Press \fBtab\fR on the watch, or \fBctrl\-space tab\fR from anywhere in the
server.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
The cursor goes to the AI that has waited longest. Its pane takes the slot and
the keys land in it.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
Answer it.
.TP
\fB4.\fR
Press \fBtab\fR again for the next, longest first, and round again from the end.
.RE
.TP
4-4.
\fBWORKING TWO HANDS.\fR \fBctrl\-space ctrl\-space\fR goes to the hand that was
last in the slot, and pressed again comes back. It is the key for a shell and
the AI being asked about it, or a build and the file it fails on. A vacant slot
and the look are not remembered. \fBalt\-o\fR does the same from the rail.
.TP
4-5.
\fBRE\-ESTABLISHING CONTACT.\fR A conversation does not end with the AI. Its
record is enough to pick it back up. To continue one:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
On the watch, put the cursor on any row of the place. Or open the list and put
the cursor on the project.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
Press \fBalt\-a\fR. The picker lists the conversations suspended at the place,
newest first, each by its branch, the last thing it was asked, and its age. Type
to narrow the rows.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
Press \fBenter\fR. A shell opens running \fBclaude \-\-resume\fR on that
conversation and takes the slot.
.TP
\fB4.\fR
\fBesc\fR returns to the watch with nothing continued.
.RE
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
A conversation a live AI is carrying is not listed. The picker checks the
process table rather than the record alone.
.TP
4-6.
\fBENDING AN AI.\fR To end an AI:
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
Put the cursor on its row and press \fBx\fR.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
CONFIRM lights on the bar, with the question beside it: END CLAUDE 1234 · X
CONFIRMS · ANY OTHER KEY CANCELS.
.TP
\fB3.\fR
Press \fBx\fR, \fBy\fR, or \fBenter\fR. The AI is asked to end. The shell it ran
in is left at its prompt. Any other key cancels.
.TP
\fB4.\fR
The watch reads again after a beat, and the row goes when the AI has.
.RE
.TP
\fBNOTE\fR
An AI ended this way keeps its record. Procedure 4-5 continues it.
.SH EMERGENCY PROCEDURES
.TP
5-1.
\fBA HAND THAT WILL NOT END.\fR \fBx\fR asks a hand to end with the signal a
well-behaved command answers by saving and shutting down. A bare shell ignores
that signal and is killed outright instead; there is nothing in it to save. A
hand that ignores the request stays on the watch. Reach it with \fBenter\fR and
end it from inside.
.TP
5-2.
\fBTAKING THE STATION DOWN.\fR To end the server and everything in it:
.TP
\fBCAUTION\fR
\fBconn down\fR ends every hand in the server: every shell, every AI, and every
run, in the slot or out of sight in a window of its own. Work that is not saved
is lost. A conversation can be re-established afterward (4-5). A run cannot.
.RS
.TP
\fB1.\fR
From a terminal outside conn, or from a shell in it, type \fBconn down\fR.
.TP
\fB2.\fR
conn reports a line for each window it ended and one for the server.
.RE
.TP
5-3.
\fBTHE SLOT EMPTIES.\fR When the hand in the slot ends, the slot takes the next
hand conn holds, from the cursor down and round again from the top. With none to
take, it shows VACANT. Any key in it puts the keys on the rail.
.TP
5-4.
\fBTHE KEYS ARE ELSEWHERE.\fR From any pane of the server, \fBctrl\-space \-\fR
puts the keys on the rail. From the rail, \fBenter\fR puts them in the slot.
.TP
5-5.
\fBTHE WATCH CANNOT READ.\fR When the process table cannot be read, the watch
shows the reason as a chip in place of its rows and reads again on the next
beat. On macOS the usual reason is that \fBlsof\fR gave no answer in time.
